## Overview

jsoup's parser subsystem (`org.jsoup.parser`, `src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/package-info.java:2`) is split into three cooperating layers: a **`Parser`** facade, a character-level **`Tokeniser`**/`TokeniserState` state machine that turns raw text into `Token`s, and a **`TreeBuilder`** (with an `HtmlTreeBuilder` insertion-mode state machine and a much simpler `XmlTreeBuilder`) that consumes those tokens and builds the DOM.

### 1. `Parser` – the public facade

`Parser` is the entry point applications use (`Parser.htmlParser()`, `Parser.xmlParser()`, `parseInput`, `parseFragmentInput`, etc.) — `src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/Parser.java:21-380`. It doesn't itself parse; it just owns configuration (settings, error tracking, max depth, `TagSet`) and delegates the actual work to a `TreeBuilder`:

```java
public Document parseInput(Reader inputHtml, String baseUri) {
    ... return treeBuilder.parse(inputHtml, baseUri, this); ...
}
```
(`src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/Parser.java:88-95`)

`Parser.htmlParser()` wires up an `HtmlTreeBuilder`, and `Parser.xmlParser()` wires up an `XmlTreeBuilder` (and relaxes the max-depth limit to `Integer.MAX_VALUE`) — `src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/Parser.java:369-380`.

### 2. `TreeBuilder` – shared parse driver

`TreeBuilder` is an abstract base class shared by both HTML and XML parsing (`src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/TreeBuilder.java:22`). It holds the shared parse state: the `CharacterReader`, the `Tokeniser`, the current `Document`, the stack of open `Element`s, current base URI, settings, and the `TagSet` (`src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/TreeBuilder.java:23-33`).

Its `initialiseParse` creates the `Document`, the `CharacterReader`, and a new `Tokeniser` bound to itself (`src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/TreeBuilder.java:41-62`), and `runParser`/`stepParser` drive the main loop: pull a `Token` from the tokeniser, hand it to the subclass's `process(Token)` method, and repeat until EOF, finally popping the remaining stack (`src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/TreeBuilder.java:105-130`):

```java
void runParser() {
    do {} while (stepParser());
    completeParse();
}
boolean stepParser() {
    ...
    final Token token = tokeniser.read();
    currentToken = token;
    process(token);
    ...
}
abstract boolean process(Token token);
```

Common bookkeeping — pushing/popping the open-element stack, enforcing the max-depth limit, tracking source ranges, error reporting, and resolving `Tag`s from the shared `TagSet` — all lives here (`src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/TreeBuilder.java:162-267`), so the two concrete builders only need to supply tree-construction semantics.

### 3. `Tokeniser` and `TokeniserState` – the lexical state machine

`Tokeniser` (`src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/Tokeniser.java:14`) reads characters from the shared `CharacterReader` and emits `Token`s (start/end tags, comments, character data, doctype, XML declarations, EOF). Internally it delegates each read to a `TokeniserState` enum value:

```java
Token read() {
    while (!isEmitPending) {
        state.read(this, reader);
    }
    ...
}
```
(`src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/Tokeniser.java:64-77`)

`TokeniserState` (`src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/TokeniserState.java:11`) is a large enum where each constant (e.g. `Data`, `TagOpen`, `MarkupDeclarationOpen`, `CommentStart`, `Doctype`, `MarkupProcessingOpen`, etc.) implements a `read(Tokeniser, CharacterReader)` method describing exactly one lexical state and its transitions, mirroring the WHATWG HTML tokeniser state machine — e.g. the `Data` state transitions to `TagOpen` on `<` (`src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/TokeniserState.java:12-34`).

Importantly, **the same `Tokeniser`/`TokeniserState` machine is used for both HTML and XML** — the tokeniser is constructed with a `syntax` flag set from the tree builder type:

```java
syntax = treeBuilder instanceof XmlTreeBuilder ? Document.OutputSettings.Syntax.xml : Document.OutputSettings.Syntax.html;
```
(`src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/Tokeniser.java:56`)

A handful of tokeniser states branch on `t.syntax == xml` to change behavior — e.g. `MarkupDeclarationOpen` treats `<!ENTITY...` as an XML declaration only in XML syntax (`src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/TokeniserState.java:940-964`), `MarkupProcessingOpen` handles `<?xml...?>` processing instructions (only reachable in XML mode) (`src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/TokeniserState.java:965-977`), and `AfterDoctypeName` recognizes an internal DTD subset `[` only for XML (`src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/TokeniserState.java:1248-1251`). So tokenising is almost entirely shared code, with small syntax-conditional branches for XML-only constructs (XML declarations, DTD internal subsets).

### 4. `HtmlTreeBuilder` + `HtmlTreeBuilderState` – the HTML insertion-mode state machine

`HtmlTreeBuilder` (`src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/HtmlTreeBuilder.java:30`) is where HTML5 tree-construction complexity lives: active formatting elements, a "frameset-ok" flag, a template insertion-mode stack, a `noscript` island state, pending table characters, and a fragment-parsing context (`src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/HtmlTreeBuilder.java:41-56`).

Its `process(Token)` doesn't implement tree construction directly — it dispatches to a current `HtmlTreeBuilderState`:

```java
protected boolean process(Token token) {
    if (noscriptState != null && state != HtmlTreeBuilderState.Text)
        return processNoscriptToken(token);
    HtmlTreeBuilderState dispatch = useCurrentOrForeignInsert(token) ? this.state : ForeignContent;
    return dispatch.process(token, this);
}
```
(`src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/HtmlTreeBuilder.java:151-157`)

`HtmlTreeBuilderState` (`src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/HtmlTreeBuilderState.java:24`) is an enum implementing the HTML5 spec's "insertion modes" as states — `Initial`, `BeforeHtml`, `BeforeHead`, `InHead`, `AfterHead`, `InBody`, `Text`, `InTable`, `InTableText`, `InCaption`, `InColumnGroup`, `InTableBody`, `InRow`, `InCell`, `InSelect`, `InSelectInTable`, `InTemplate`, `AfterBody`, `InFrameset`, `AfterFrameset`, `AfterAfterBody`, `AfterAfterFrameset`, and `ForeignContent` (for foreign SVG/MathML content) (`src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/HtmlTreeBuilderState.java:25-1746`). Each state's `process(Token, HtmlTreeBuilder)` implements the exact HTML5 algorithm for that mode, e.g. `Initial` handles doctype parsing and quirks-mode detection before transitioning to `BeforeHtml` (`src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/HtmlTreeBuilderState.java:25-51`). States transition via `tb.transition(...)`, and can also "reprocess" the same token in a new state (`return tb.process(t);`), a standard pattern from the WHATWG algorithm (`src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/HtmlTreeBuilderState.java:46-79`).

`useCurrentOrForeignInsert` implements the spec's "adjusted current node" rule to decide whether the current token should be processed by the ordinary insertion-mode state or diverted into `ForeignContent` handling for MathML/SVG islands (`src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/HtmlTreeBuilder.java:233-270`). HTML tags also carry semantic metadata used by this state machine and by `TreeBuilder.tagFor`, via the `Tag` class — e.g. whether a tag is block/inline, void/empty, self-closing, whitespace-preserving, or has a special tokeniser "text state" for elements like `<script>`/`<title>` (`src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/Tag.java:260-344`), and this is looked up from a shared, mutable `TagSet` (`src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/TreeBuilder.java:261-267`, `src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/HtmlTreeBuilder.java:58-60` sets `ParseSettings.htmlDefault`).

### 5. `XmlTreeBuilder` – no insertion-mode state machine

By contrast, `XmlTreeBuilder` (`src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/XmlTreeBuilder.java:37`) has no equivalent state-machine layer. Its `process(Token)` is a flat `switch` on token type that inserts nodes directly and structurally, with no notion of "insertion modes", implied tags, formatting-element reconstruction, or foreign-content rules:

```java
protected boolean process(Token token) {
    currentToken = token;
    switch (token.type) {
        case StartTag: insertElementFor(token.asStartTag()); break;
        case EndTag:   popStackToClose(token.asEndTag()); break;
        case Comment:  insertCommentFor(token.asComment()); break;
        case Character: insertCharacterFor(token.asCharacter()); break;
        case Doctype:  insertDoctypeFor(token.asDoctype()); break;
        case XmlDecl:  insertXmlDeclarationFor(token.asXmlDecl()); break;
        case EOF: break;
        default: Validate.fail(...);
    }
    return true;
}
```
(`src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/XmlTreeBuilder.java:114-144`)

XML parsing instead does its own lightweight namespace resolution per element, maintaining a stack of prefix→URI maps and processing `xmlns`/`xmlns:*` attributes as each start tag is inserted (`src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/XmlTreeBuilder.java:146-218`), something the HTML side does not need since it works from a fixed, known-semantics tag set rather than aribtrary user-declared elements/namespaces.

Other concrete differences enforced at the `TreeBuilder`/`Parser` level:
- Default settings: HTML uses `ParseSettings.htmlDefault` (case-insensitive) vs. XML's `ParseSettings.preserveCase` (`src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/HtmlTreeBuilder.java:58-60`, `src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/XmlTreeBuilder.java:42-44`).
- Default namespace: HTML defaults to `NamespaceHtml` (`src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/TreeBuilder.java:273-275`), XML overrides to `NamespaceXml` (`src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/XmlTreeBuilder.java:100-102`).
- Default `TagSet`: HTML uses the built-in `TagSet.Html()` catalogue with all HTML tag semantics (`src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/TreeBuilder.java:277-279`), while XML starts from an empty `TagSet` since it has no built-in notion of tag semantics (`src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/XmlTreeBuilder.java:104-107`).
- Default max stack depth: HTML defaults to 512 open elements (`src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/TreeBuilder.java:198-203`), while XML overrides this to unlimited (`src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/XmlTreeBuilder.java:109-112`, also set explicitly in `Parser.xmlParser()` at `src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/Parser.java:378-380`).

### Summary of the structure

- **`Parser`** — public API/config wrapper, delegates to a `TreeBuilder`.
- **`Tokeniser` + `TokeniserState`** — a single shared lexical state machine (driven by `CharacterReader`) that converts characters into `Token`s; a `syntax` flag toggles a small number of XML-specific lexical rules (declarations, internal DTD subset) inside otherwise shared states (`src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/Tokeniser.java:56`, `src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/TokeniserState.java:954`, `:1248`).
- **`TreeBuilder`** — shared parse driver/stack/error-tracking logic (`src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/TreeBuilder.java:22-343`), extended by:
  - **`HtmlTreeBuilder`**, which delegates all tree-construction decisions to the **`HtmlTreeBuilderState`** insertion-mode state machine (`src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/HtmlTreeBuilder.java:151-157`, `src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/HtmlTreeBuilderState.java:24-1853`), implementing full HTML5 semantics (implicit tags, formatting reconstruction, table/foreign-content quirks).
  - **`XmlTreeBuilder`**, which has no insertion-mode machine at all — it maps tokens straight to node construction in one flat switch, and instead layers on its own namespace-resolution logic per element (`src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/XmlTreeBuilder.java:114-218`).

