## The conflict-resolution code: `DataUtil.detectCharset`

```java
private static CharsetDoc detectCharset(ControllableInputStream input, @Nullable String charsetName, String baseUri, Parser parser, boolean reuseDocIfFullyRead) throws IOException {
    Document doc = null;
    // look for BOM - overrides any other header or input
    String bomCharset = detectCharsetFromBom(input);
    if (bomCharset != null)
        charsetName = bomCharset;

    if (charsetName == null) { // read ahead and determine from meta. safe first parse as UTF-8
        ...
        try (Reader reader = new SimpleStreamReader(input, UTF_8)) {
            doc = parser.parseInput(reader, baseUri);
            input.reset();
            input.max(origMax);
        } ...

        // look for <meta http-equiv="Content-Type" ...> / <meta charset=...>
        Elements metaElements = doc.select(metaCharset);
        String foundCharset = null;
        for (Element meta : metaElements) { ... }

        // look for <?xml encoding='ISO-8859-1'?>
        if (foundCharset == null && doc.childNodeSize() > 0) { ... }

        foundCharset = validateCharset(foundCharset);
        if (foundCharset != null && !foundCharset.equalsIgnoreCase(defaultCharsetName)) { // need to re-decode.
            foundCharset = foundCharset.trim().replaceAll("[\"']", "");
            charsetName = foundCharset;
            doc = null;                 // <-- triggers a full re-parse
        } else if (reuseDocIfFullyRead && input.baseReadFully()) {
            input.close();
        } else {
            doc = null;
        }
    } else { // specified by content type header (or by user on file load)
        Validate.notEmpty(charsetName, "Must set charset arg ...");
    }
    ...
}
```
(`src/main/java/org/jsoup/helper/DataUtil.java:251-321`)

Key points from this code:

- **BOM override**: `detectCharsetFromBom` is always called first, unconditionally overwriting `charsetName` — this happens *before* the check for whether a charset was already supplied, so a BOM beats both a caller-supplied charset and an HTTP-derived one. (`src/main/java/org/jsoup/helper/DataUtil.java:254-257`, BOM detection logic at `406-425`)
- **Only sniffed when nothing was supplied**: The whole meta-tag/XML-declaration scanning block only executes `if (charsetName == null)` (`DataUtil.java:259`). If the caller or the HTTP response already supplied a charset, that `else` branch is taken instead, which does nothing but validate that the string isn't empty (`DataUtil.java:312-314`) — the document's own declared charset is never consulted, so a caller/HTTP-supplied charset simply overrides any conflicting in-document declaration.
- **Re-parse trigger**: When no charset was supplied, jsoup does a first pass parsing the stream as UTF-8 (`DataUtil.java:265-266`), scans for a declared charset in `<meta>` tags or an XML declaration (`DataUtil.java:275-301`), and if it finds one that differs from the UTF-8 default, it sets `charsetName = foundCharset; doc = null;` (`DataUtil.java:303-306`). Setting `doc = null` is the actual trigger — it means the `CharsetDoc.doc` field returned from `detectCharset` will be `null`.

## Where the re-parse actually happens

```java
static Document parseInputStream(CharsetDoc charsetDoc, String baseUri, Parser parser) throws IOException {
    // if doc != null it was fully parsed during charset detection; so just return that
    if (charsetDoc.doc != null)
        return charsetDoc.doc;

    final InputStream input = charsetDoc.input;
    ...
    final Charset charset = charsetDoc.charset;
    try (Reader reader = new SimpleStreamReader(input, charset)) {
        try {
            doc = parser.parseInput(reader, baseUri);
        } ...
        doc.outputSettings().charset(charset);
        if (!charset.canEncode()) {
            doc.charset(UTF_8);
        }
    }
    return doc;
}
```
(`src/main/java/org/jsoup/helper/DataUtil.java:323-346`)

Because `charsetDoc.doc` is `null` (set at `DataUtil.java:306`), this method skips the short-circuit at `DataUtil.java:325-326` and rewinds/re-reads the stream (`SimpleStreamReader`) using the newly detected charset, calling `parser.parseInput(reader, baseUri)` a second time — this is the actual re-parse.

## Summary

- **BOM present** → always wins, no meta-tag sniffing happens at all (`DataUtil.java:254-257`, `406-425`).
- **No charset supplied by caller/HTTP** → jsoup does a first UTF-8 pass, looks for a declared charset, and if a different one is found, discards that first parse and re-parses the stream with the discovered charset (`DataUtil.java:259-311`, re-parse executed in `DataUtil.java:323-346`).
- **Charset supplied by caller or HTTP `Content-Type`** → that value is used unconditionally; the in-document declared charset is never even inspected, so there is no re-parse and no explicit "conflict" resolution beyond simply trusting the supplied value (`DataUtil.java:312-314`, supplied from `HttpConnection.java:976` and consumed via `HttpConnection.java:1051`).
